WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.475
"I suppose your stepfather, Mr. Windibank, was very annoyed that you went to the ball," Sherlock Holmes said to our client.

2
00:00:06.476 --> 00:00:10.546
"No, he was actually very good about it," said Mary Sutherland.

3
00:00:10.547 --> 00:00:13.938
"As I remember, he just laughed and shrugged his shoulders.

4
00:00:13.939 --> 00:00:18.372
He said there was no use in denying anything to a woman because she would always get her way."

5
00:00:18.372 --> 00:00:22.617
"So what happened after you met Hosmer Angel at the Plumbers' Ball?" I asked.

6
00:00:22.618 --> 00:00:29.767
"The next day Mr. Angel came to call on my mother and me. My stepfather was still in France on business," said Miss Sutherland.

7
00:00:29.768 --> 00:00:37.115
"I met Mr. Angel twice for walks. But after that my stepfather returned, so Mr. Angel couldn't come to the house anymore."

8
00:00:37.116 --> 00:00:42.112
This puzzled me. "Why couldn't he visit you?" I asked.

9
00:00:42.113 --> 00:00:46.024
"Well, my stepfather didn't like me to have visitors," said Miss Sutherland.

10
00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:52.439
"He said a woman should be happy with her own family. But I used to say to Mother that I didn't have my own family yet!"

11
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.018
"Did Mr. Angel make an attempt to see you?" asked Holmes.

12
00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:57.931
"No, but he wrote to me."

13
00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:01.847
At that point Miss Sutherland's stepfather was going back to France in a week.

14
00:01:01.848 --> 00:01:06.505
Mr. Angel wrote and said it would be safer not to see her until her stepfather had gone.

15
00:01:06.506 --> 00:01:10.245
In the meantime he suggested that they write to each other every day.

16
00:01:10.246 --> 00:01:17.585
Miss Sutherland was the first person in her family to see the mail each morning, so she didn't fear that Mr. Windibank would find Mr. Angel's letters.

17
00:01:17.586 --> 00:01:21.657
"Were you engaged to the gentleman at the time?" asked Holmes.

18
00:01:21.658 --> 00:01:29.898
"Oh yes. We were engaged after the first walk that we took. Mr. Angel was a cashier in an office on Leadenhall Street and—"

19
00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:32.398
"What office?" Holmes interrupted.

20
00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:37.320
"That's the worst of it, Mr. Holmes," replied Miss Sutherland. "I don't know."

21
00:01:37.321 --> 00:01:40.233
"Then where did he live?" asked the detective.

22
00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:42.225
"He slept at the office."

23
00:01:42.226 --> 00:01:46.061
"Let me make sure I understand the situation," said Holmes.

24
00:01:46.062 --> 00:01:52.457
"You didn't know the name of Mr. Angel's office or its exact address. So how did you address letters to him?"

25
00:01:52.458 --> 00:01:57.124
"I sent them to the Leadenhall Street post office to be left there until he called for them."

26
00:01:57.125 --> 00:02:00.965
Miss Sutherland went on to say that Mr. Angel thought it was better that way.

27
00:02:00.966 --> 00:02:06.045
He didn't want the other clerks in his office to tease him about getting letters from a lady.

28
00:02:06.046 --> 00:02:10.292
Miss Sutherland offered to type her letters, so no one would see her feminine handwriting.

29
00:02:10.293 --> 00:02:15.696
But Mr. Angel didn't like the idea of her using a typewriter, even though he typed all his letters to her.

30
00:02:15.697 --> 00:02:20.781
"He said that when I wrote the letters, they seemed to come directly from me," said Miss Sutherland.

31
00:02:20.782 --> 00:02:24.789
"But when they were typed, he always felt the machine had come between us. That

32
00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:28.866
just shows you how fond he was of me and the little things that he would think of." "That

33
00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:36.430
is most revealing," said Holmes. "It has long been a belief of mine that the little things are always the most important. Can

34
00:02:36.431 --> 00:02:39.851
you remember any other little things about Mr. Hosmer Angel?" "He

35
00:02:39.852 --> 00:02:48.822
was a very shy man, Mr. Holmes. He would rather walk with me in the evening than in the daylight because he hated to be noticed by anyone," said Miss Sutherland. "He

36
00:02:48.823 --> 00:02:53.084
was very modest and kind. Even his voice was gentle." Mr.

37
00:02:53.085 --> 00:02:57.581
Angel had suffered from a severe sore throat and swollen glands as a youth. As

38
00:02:57.582 --> 00:03:03.915
a result he spoke in a hesitant whisper. He was always well dressed, very neat and plain. "But

39
00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:10.396
his eyes were weak just like mine," said Miss Sutherland. "He wore tinted glasses to protect them from the light."

40
00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:14.078
"What happened when your stepfather returned from France?" I asked.

41
00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:17.880
"Mr. Angel suggested that we should marry before my stepfather came back.

42
00:03:17.881 --> 00:03:23.896
Mr. Angel was dreadfully serious and made me swear on the Bible that I would always be true to him no matter what happened."

43
00:03:23.897 --> 00:03:27.398
"What did your mother think about all this?" asked Holmes.

44
00:03:27.399 --> 00:03:31.803
"Mother said he was quite right to make me swear my love on the Bible.

45
00:03:31.804 --> 00:03:35.621
She said it was a sign of his passion for me," said Miss Sutherland.

46
00:03:35.622 --> 00:03:40.050
"She approved of him right from the beginning and was even fonder of him than I was."

47
00:03:40.051 --> 00:03:46.368
When Mr. Angel talked about getting married within a week, Miss Sutherland began to worry about what her stepfather would think.

48
00:03:46.369 --> 00:03:50.797
Both Mr. Angel and her mother said not to be concerned about Mr. Windibank, though.

49
00:03:50.798 --> 00:03:54.460
It would be fine to tell him about the wedding after it took place.

50
00:03:54.461 --> 00:03:57.690
"I didn't quite like that," Miss Sutherland confessed.

51
00:03:57.691 --> 00:04:02.857
"It seemed odd to ask for my stepfather's permission to wed because he was only a few years older than I.

52
00:04:02.858 --> 00:04:06.272
But I also didn't want to keep any secrets from my stepfather."

53
00:04:06.273 --> 00:04:11.780
So she wrote to Mr. Windibank in Bordeaux, France, where his company had its office.

54
00:04:11.781 --> 00:04:15.354
However the letter was returned to her unopened on the morning of the wedding.

55
00:04:15.355 --> 00:04:18.766
"Then your stepfather didn't receive the letter?" asked Holmes.

56
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:23.924
"Apparently he had already left for England just before it arrived in France," said Miss Sutherland.

57
00:04:23.925 --> 00:04:30.497
"The preparations went on for a quiet ceremony. On the morning of the wedding, Mr. Angel arrived at our house by cab."

58
00:04:30.498 --> 00:04:38.575
There was room for only two people in the cab, so he put Miss Sutherland and her mother into the vehicle. Then he stepped into another cab right behind them.

59
00:04:38.576 --> 00:04:45.002
"We got to the church first, and when Mr. Angel's cab arrived, we waited for him to step out," said Miss Sutherland.

60
00:04:45.003 --> 00:04:50.985
"But he never did. When the cabman climbed down and looked inside the cab, no one was there!

61
00:04:50.986 --> 00:04:57.309
The cabman could not imagine what had happened to Mr. Angel because he had seen my fiancé get into the cab with his own eyes.

62
00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.976
That was last Friday, and I have not heard from Mr. Angel since."

63
00:05:00.977 --> 00:05:06.809
"It seems to me that you have been shamefully treated," said Holmes. "Mr. Angel abandoned you."

64
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:11.202
"Oh no, sir! Mr. Angel was too good and kind to abandon me."

65
00:05:11.203 --> 00:05:16.558
All that morning her fiancé had been urging her to promise that she'd be true to him no matter what happened.

66
00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:24.297
Even if something unforeseen should separate them, she was to remember that she was pledged to him. And that sooner or later he would return for her.

67
00:05:24.298 --> 00:05:31.876
"It seemed strange talk for a wedding morning," admitted Miss Sutherland. "But what happened afterward gave meaning to it."

68
00:05:31.877 --> 00:05:36.534
"So you think that Mr. Angel met with some catastrophe?" Holmes asked Miss Sutherland.

69
00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:45.186
"Yes, sir. I believe that Mr. Angel expected some danger, or else he wouldn't have talked like that. And then I think that what he feared actually happened."

70
00:05:45.187 --> 00:05:48.440
"How did your mother take his disappearance?" I asked.

71
00:05:48.441 --> 00:05:51.955
"She was angry and said I was never to speak of the matter again."

72
00:05:51.956 --> 00:05:55.193
"And your stepfather," I said, "did you tell him?"

73
00:05:55.194 --> 00:06:00.265
"Yes, and he agreed with me that something must have happened to Mr. Angel," Miss Sutherland said.

74
00:06:00.266 --> 00:06:08.083
She wiped her eyes with her handkerchief. "But my stepfather thought I would hear from my dear Hosmer again."

